PAGES = [
  {'slug': 'ai', 'name': 'AI & Automation', 'title': 'AI & Automation for Dorset Businesses | 365 Techies', 'desc': 'AI and automation from a family-run Bournemouth IT team. Working demos, straightforward monthly service pricing and human control on every system we build.', 'eyebrow': 'AI &amp; Automation', 'h1': 'AI and automation that take the repetitive work off your team', 'hero_lede': 'We design, build and run practical AI and automation for Bournemouth, Poole and Dorset businesses &mdash; and we run the same systems in our own company every day. Describe the job that eats your team&rsquo;s week and we&rsquo;ll tell you honestly whether AI can do it.', 'chips': ['Family-run since 1995', '4.9-star Google rating', 'We run these systems ourselves'], 'cta_primary': {'label': "Tell us what's wasting your team's time", 'href': '/ai/start/'}, 'cta_secondary': {'label': 'Try the demo', 'href': '/365-ai-os/'}, 'sections': [{'h2': 'The same local team that looks after your IT', 'html': '<p>365 Techies is a family-run IT support business, trading in Bournemouth since 1995, with a 4.9-star Google rating. We&rsquo;re a Microsoft Partner and a Victron Recommended Software Integrator, and every AI system we build is supported by the same local team that looks after your computers, email and network &mdash; not a faceless platform. We serve Bournemouth, Poole, Christchurch and the wider Dorset area, and work remotely UK-wide where that suits.</p>'}, {'h2': 'Start with the problem, not the technology', 'html': '<p>You don&rsquo;t need to know what an &ldquo;AI agent&rdquo; is to talk to us. Find the problem that sounds like yours and we&rsquo;ll take it from there.</p><ul><li><strong>Enquiries pile up and quotes go out late.</strong> Automation can log every enquiry, draft the reply and chase the quote &mdash; see <a href="/ai/automations/">AI automations</a>.</li><li><strong>The phone rings more than you can answer.</strong> An <a href="/ai/voice-agents/">AI voice agent</a> answers politely, takes the details and books the job, day or night.</li><li><strong>Someone retypes the same information all day.</strong> Data entry between systems is the first thing <a href="/ai/automations/">automation</a> removes.</li><li><strong>Documents arrive half-finished and someone has to chase.</strong> An automation can read what came in, spot what&rsquo;s missing and request it.</li><li><strong>Reports take a day to assemble.</strong> Automated reporting pulls the numbers together on schedule, the same way every time.</li><li><strong>Your systems don&rsquo;t talk to each other.</strong> We connect them &mdash; with an <a href="/ai/agents/">AI agent</a> where judgement is needed, plain automation where it isn&rsquo;t.</li><li><strong>Not sure where AI fits?</strong> That&rsquo;s the most common starting point. <a href="/ai/consultancy/">AI consultancy</a> starts from your processes, not a product.</li></ul>'}, {'h2': 'Five ways we put AI to work', 'html': '<ul><li><a href="/ai/automations/"><strong>AI automations</strong></a> &mdash; joined-up workflows that move information between your systems automatically, with AI used only for the steps that genuinely need it.</li><li><a href="/ai/agents/"><strong>AI agents</strong></a> &mdash; software that can read, decide and act across several systems to complete a whole task, with human approval where it matters.</li><li><a href="/ai/voice-agents/"><strong>AI voice agents</strong></a> &mdash; a natural-sounding assistant that answers your phone, takes messages and books appointments, so callers never hit voicemail.</li><li><a href="/ai/consultancy/"><strong>AI consultancy</strong></a> &mdash; a structured look at where AI genuinely helps your business, and where it doesn&rsquo;t.</li><li><a href="/ai/training/"><strong>AI training</strong></a> &mdash; practical sessions so your team can use AI tools confidently and safely.</li></ul>'}, {'h2': 'How we work: from problem to running system', 'html': '<p>Every project follows the same six steps. <strong>Problem</strong> &mdash; you describe the job in your own words. <strong>Scope</strong> &mdash; we map it and quote the build by complexity, so you see the full figure before anything starts. <strong>Build</strong> &mdash; we design and build the system, testing it against real examples from your business. <strong>Safeguard</strong> &mdash; approval steps, records and sensible limits go in before it goes live. <strong>Measure</strong> &mdash; we agree what &ldquo;working&rdquo; means and check against it. <strong>Support</strong> &mdash; your monthly service keeps it hosted, monitored and up to date, with a local team who answer when something changes.</p><p>Commercially it&rsquo;s two parts: a one-off design and build, quoted by complexity, plus a monthly service subscription that keeps the system running and watched. No tiers, no surprises.</p>'}, {'h2': 'Works with the systems you already use', 'html': '<p>We connect AI and automation to the tools most Dorset businesses already run: Microsoft 365, email, calendars, CRMs, accounting software, booking systems and phone systems. We won&rsquo;t show you a wall of logos. The honest answer is that if a system offers a way in, we can usually connect it &mdash; and if it doesn&rsquo;t, we&rsquo;ll tell you at scoping, not after you&rsquo;ve paid for a build.</p>'}, {'h2': 'A human stays in charge', 'html': '<p>AI is used only for the steps where it genuinely helps &mdash; reading a message, drafting a reply, spotting what&rsquo;s missing. Everything else runs on plain, deterministic automation that does the same thing every time. For consequential actions &mdash; sending a quote, changing a booking, contacting a customer &mdash; we can add a human approval step, so nothing important happens without someone on your team saying yes. Every system keeps a record of what it did, so you can always check its work.</p>'}, {'h2': 'Proof you can actually see', 'html': '<p>We&rsquo;d rather show you than tell you. The <a href="/365-ai-os/">365 AI OS</a> is a working Claude-powered AI desktop we built, with a live demo on this site &mdash; a scripted demonstration, and honestly labelled as one. Beyond the demo, the automations we sell are the ones we run in our own business every day: booking automation, review-request automation, SMS scheduling and Slack-connected workflows, alongside our own <strong>365 PC Manager</strong> Windows app, a live customer portal and real-time monitoring dashboards. We haven&rsquo;t published AI customer case studies yet, and we won&rsquo;t invent any &mdash; what you see running is what we actually built.</p>'}, {'h2': 'Where our proof already lives', 'html': '<p>Three kinds of business map straight onto systems we already run or have built:</p><ul><li><strong>Trades and service businesses</strong> &mdash; booking, quoting, reminders and follow-up automation is exactly what we run for ourselves.</li><li><strong>Marine and off-grid energy</strong> &mdash; we&rsquo;re a Victron Recommended Software Integrator, building live telemetry dashboards for boats and vehicles.</li><li><strong>Office-based firms</strong> &mdash; document handling, reporting and Microsoft 365 automation, delivered by a Microsoft Partner.</li></ul>'}, {'h2': 'What shapes your quote', 'html': '<p>Build quotes are set by complexity, not a rate card. The main factors: how many systems we&rsquo;re connecting, how much volume flows through them, whether a human approval step is needed, how tidy your existing data is, and whether your team needs a screen to work in or something that runs quietly in the background. Scoping settles all of this before you commit &mdash; <a href="/contact/">request an AI quote</a> and we&rsquo;ll come back with a figure you can plan around.</p>'}, {'h2': 'Not ready to talk yet?', 'html': '<p>Fair enough. <a href="/365-ai-os/">Try the AI OS demo</a> to see the technology working, browse our <a href="/ai/tools/">free online tools</a>, or look at <a href="/ai/training/">AI training</a> if you&rsquo;d rather build confidence in-house first. When a job in your business starts to look automatable, you&rsquo;ll know where we are.</p>'}, {'h2': 'Tell us what&rsquo;s wasting your team&rsquo;s time', 'html': '<p>You don&rsquo;t need a specification, a budget or the right vocabulary &mdash; just describe the job that eats your team&rsquo;s week. We&rsquo;ll tell you honestly whether AI helps, what plain automation could do instead, and what a build would cost if it does. <a href="/contact/">Talk to us about AI</a> &mdash; a real conversation with the local team that would actually build it.</p>'}], 'faqs': [{'q': 'How much does AI automation actually cost?', 'a': '<p>Every AI service has two parts: a one-off design and build, quoted by complexity once we&rsquo;ve scoped the job, and a monthly service subscription covering hosting, monitoring, updates and support &mdash; confirmed with your quote. You see the full figure before anything is built, and if the honest answer is that automation won&rsquo;t pay for itself in your case, we&rsquo;ll say so.</p>'}, {'q': 'Do we need to be technical to use any of this?', 'a': '<p>No. You describe the problem in plain English; we choose the technology, build it and run it. You never have to pick between tools or learn the jargon. If your team wants to get more from AI tools themselves, our <a href="/ai/training/">AI training</a> covers that side.</p>'}, {'q': 'What happens when the AI gets something wrong?', 'a': '<p>We plan for it rather than pretend it won&rsquo;t happen. AI handles only the steps where it genuinely helps; predictable, deterministic automation does the rest. Consequential actions can require human approval before they happen, every system keeps a record of what it did, and monitoring is part of your monthly service &mdash; so problems are caught and fixed by a local team, not lost in a ticket queue.</p>'}, {'q': 'Will it work with the software we already use?', 'a': '<p>Usually, yes. We routinely connect Microsoft 365, email, calendars, CRMs, accounting packages, booking systems and phone systems. We check exactly what your systems allow during scoping and tell you before you spend anything &mdash; not after.</p>'}, {'q': 'Can we see it working before we commit to anything?', 'a': '<p>Yes. The <a href="/365-ai-os/">365 AI OS demo</a> runs live on this site &mdash; it&rsquo;s a scripted demonstration and clearly labelled as one. Beyond that, our strongest evidence is that we run these automations in our own business every day: bookings, review requests, SMS scheduling and Slack-connected workflows all run on systems we built ourselves.</p>'}, {'q': 'Where does our data go?', 'a': '<p>It depends on the build, and we map it with you at scoping: which systems hold your data, which steps an AI model actually sees, and what stays entirely inside your existing accounts. Nothing goes live until you know exactly what connects to what &mdash; data protection is part of the design conversation from the start, not an afterthought.</p>'}], 'final_cta': {'h2': 'Start with the problem, not the technology', 'lede': 'Tell us what eats your team&rsquo;s week &mdash; we&rsquo;ll show you what a connected, human-controlled automation would change, and exactly how the scope is priced.'}, 'pricing': {'subscription_line': 'Monthly service — confirmed with your quote', 'build_line': 'One-off design & build — quoted by complexity', 'note': 'Two parts, no tiers: a monthly service subscription that keeps your system hosted, monitored and supported, plus a one-off build quoted once the job is scoped. You see the full figure before anything is built.'}, 'service_desc': 'Practical AI and automation services for small and medium businesses - workflow automation, AI agents, voice agents, consultancy and training, built and supported in Dorset.'},
  {'slug': 'ai/automations', 'name': 'AI Automations', 'title': 'Workflow Automation for Dorset Businesses | 365 Techies', 'desc': 'Connect your systems and remove repetitive admin. Workflow automation built and supported in Bournemouth by a team that runs it in their own business.', 'eyebrow': 'AI &amp; Automation', 'h1': 'Workflow automation that removes the repetitive work', 'hero_lede': 'If your team copies enquiries between systems, chases missing information and re-keys the same details twice, that&rsquo;s work a well-built workflow can do instead. We design, build and run automations for Dorset businesses &mdash; and we run the same kind in our own business every day.', 'chips': ['Trading since 1995', '4.9-star Google rating', 'Run in our own business daily'], 'cta_primary': {'label': "Tell us what's wasting your team's time", 'href': '/ai/start/'}, 'cta_secondary': {'label': 'Try the demo', 'href': '/365-ai-os/'}, 'sections': [{'h2': 'The repetitive work we remove', 'html': '<p>Somewhere in your business, someone is copying an enquiry out of an inbox and typing it into another system. Someone else is emailing a customer for the third time to ask for a document. At month end, someone spends an afternoon pulling numbers out of three places to build the same report they built last month.</p><p>None of this is skilled work. It is relay work &mdash; moving information from where it lands to where it needs to be &mdash; and it quietly absorbs hours every week while the jobs that actually earn money wait. Workflow automation means building a system that does the relay for you: reliably, immediately, and without forgetting. It is the most practical place to start with <a href="/ai/">AI and automation</a>, because the problems are easy to recognise and the results are easy to check.</p>'}, {'h2': 'Proof before promises: the automations we run ourselves', 'html': '<p>We are cautious about promises, so here is our evidence: we run these automations in our own business every day. When a customer books a service, the booking flows into our diary and our records without anyone re-typing it &mdash; and if someone starts a booking but walks away, a polite follow-up goes out automatically. When a job is marked complete, a review request is queued and sent at a sensible hour. New enquiries arrive in our Slack channels with the details already attached. Our <a href="/365-ai-os/">365 AI OS demo</a> shows the same thinking applied to a working AI desktop &mdash; a scripted demonstration, honestly labelled, that you can try before you talk to us.</p><p>We built all of this in-house and we support it ourselves. It has to work, because we depend on it &mdash; and that is the standard your build gets.</p>'}, {'h2': 'Before and after: one enquiry, two ways', 'html': '<p><strong>Before.</strong> A form enquiry arrives by email on Tuesday morning. It sits until someone has a minute. They read it, copy the details into the job system, and notice the customer left no phone number. They email to ask, mean to chase it, and get pulled onto something else. On Friday the customer, still waiting, calls someone else.</p><p><strong>After.</strong> The same enquiry creates a record in your system the moment it arrives. A check spots the missing phone number and sends a courteous request straight back, with a reminder two days later if nothing comes in. When the customer replies, the record updates itself and the right person gets a notification with everything attached &mdash; original message, contact details, history. The human attention the enquiry needs drops from a scattered afternoon to a couple of minutes, spent on the one decision only a person can make: do we want this job, and what do we quote?</p>'}, {'h2': 'Four workflows we build most often', 'html': '<p>Most of what we build falls into four patterns. Yours will be tailored to your systems, but these show the shape of the work.</p><ul><li><strong>Enquiry to action.</strong> A new enquiry &mdash; web form, email, or a note from a call &mdash; is read, categorised and turned into a record in your system. An acknowledgement goes to the customer at once, and the right person on your team is notified with the details already in place. AI helps with one step: reading the free-text message and pulling out what matters. Fixed rules handle everything else.</li><li><strong>Missing-information chasing.</strong> The workflow knows what a complete job, file or application needs. When something is missing &mdash; a document, a signature, a reference &mdash; it asks for it, reminds politely on a schedule you set, and stops the moment the item arrives. Nobody has to remember to chase, so nothing is chased twice and nothing is forgotten.</li><li><strong>Quote and admin orchestration.</strong> When a quote is accepted, the follow-on admin happens in order without re-keying: the job is scheduled, confirmations go out, the paperwork is raised, and your records stay in step across quoting, calendar and invoicing. One event triggers the chain, and the chain does not skip steps.</li><li><strong>Management reporting.</strong> The figures you look at every week or month are collected from the systems that hold them and assembled into the same report, on schedule, without an afternoon of copy-and-paste. Where it helps, AI adds a short plain-English summary of what changed &mdash; clearly labelled as generated, with the underlying numbers always shown.</li></ul>'}, {'h2': 'Where plain rules do the work — and where AI helps', 'html': '<p>An honest split matters here. Most of a good workflow is deterministic automation &mdash; fixed rules and code that behave exactly the same way every time. Moving data between systems, checking whether a field is filled in, sending a message at a set time: these must be exact, so we build them with rules, not AI. Rules are faster, cheaper to run, and never have an off day.</p><p>AI earns its place only at the steps that involve reading or writing ordinary language: understanding what a free-text enquiry is actually about, drafting a reply for a person to approve, summarising a month of figures into three sentences. If a step does not need AI, we do not use it. And when a job needs AI to carry more of the load &mdash; holding a conversation, working through a task with judgement &mdash; that is an <a href="/ai/agents/">AI agent</a>, which we build and price as its own service.</p>'}, {'h2': 'Exceptions, approvals and retries', 'html': '<p>Automation is only trustworthy if you can see and control what it does, so three safeguards are part of every build rather than optional extras.</p><ul><li><strong>Approvals.</strong> Any consequential action &mdash; a message to a customer, a change to a record that matters &mdash; can be routed through a human first. The workflow prepares the work; a person clicks to release it.</li><li><strong>Exceptions.</strong> When a workflow meets something it does not recognise, it stops and tells a person instead of guessing. An unusual enquiry lands with your team, flagged, with everything gathered so far.</li><li><strong>Retries and logs.</strong> If a step fails &mdash; a system briefly offline, an email bouncing &mdash; it retries sensibly and alerts us if it keeps failing. Every run is logged, so there is always an answer to &ldquo;what happened to that enquiry?&rdquo;</li></ul>'}, {'h2': 'The systems we typically connect', 'html': '<p>We connect the systems you already have rather than asking you to change them. Typical connections for Dorset businesses include Microsoft 365 email and calendars, website contact forms, booking systems, spreadsheets, accounting packages, CRMs (customer relationship management systems &mdash; the software that holds your customer records), SMS, and Slack or Teams. As a Microsoft Partner supporting Microsoft 365 every day, that side is home ground for us.</p><p>Some systems offer a proper API &mdash; a built-in way for software to exchange information directly &mdash; and some only offer exports or an email inbox. We work with either; it simply affects how the workflow is built and what it costs. You never need to pick a technology: describe the workflow, and choosing the right tools is our job.</p>'}, {'h2': 'How we measure whether it is working', 'html': '<p>Before we build anything, we agree what &ldquo;working&rdquo; means in numbers you can check yourself:</p><ul><li><strong>Response time</strong> &mdash; how long a new enquiry waits before the customer hears something.</li><li><strong>Manual touches</strong> &mdash; how many times a person handles a job&rsquo;s admin between enquiry and invoice.</li><li><strong>Missed follow-ups</strong> &mdash; chases that should have happened and did not.</li><li><strong>Reporting time</strong> &mdash; the hours spent assembling regular reports by hand.</li></ul><p>We take a baseline before the workflow goes live and compare afterwards. We will not promise a percentage saving before we have seen your workflow &mdash; anyone who does is guessing &mdash; but you will know, from your own numbers, whether it is earning its keep.</p>'}, {'h2': 'What shapes your quote', 'html': '<p>Every build is quoted individually, and the quote is shaped by a short list of factors you can sanity-check yourself: how many systems are involved and how well they connect; how much volume flows through the workflow; how many exception paths need handling; whether approval steps are required; and what reporting you want on top. A two-system workflow with one happy path is a small build. A chain that orchestrates quoting, scheduling, paperwork and invoicing across four systems is a larger one.</p><p>The pattern is always the same: a monthly service subscription that keeps the workflow hosted, monitored and supported by the same local team that looks after your IT, plus a one-off design and build, quoted by complexity, in writing, before work starts. <a href="/contact/">Tell us what&rsquo;s wasting your team&rsquo;s time</a> and we will map the workflow with you.</p>'}], 'faqs': [{'q': 'Do we have to replace our existing software?', 'a': '<p>No &mdash; workflow automation is about connecting what you already have, not replacing it. We build around your current email, forms, calendar, accounts package and job systems. If a system is genuinely holding you back we will say so, but that is a separate conversation and never a condition of the work.</p>'}, {'q': 'What happens when the automation gets something wrong?', 'a': '<p>It depends where. Steps built on fixed rules behave the same way every time, so errors there are rare and show up in the logs. Steps that involve AI &mdash; reading a message, drafting a reply &mdash; can be routed through human approval, so a person checks before anything consequential leaves the building. And when a workflow meets something it does not recognise, it is built to stop and ask rather than guess. If something does go wrong, you call the same local team that built it.</p>'}, {'q': 'Is this AI? We are wary of it.', 'a': '<p>Mostly it is not, and that is deliberate. The bulk of every workflow is deterministic automation &mdash; plain rules and code that behave the same way every time. AI is used only at steps where reading or writing ordinary language genuinely helps, and we are happy to build workflows with no AI in them at all. You can see how we use AI openly in our <a href="/365-ai-os/">365 AI OS demo</a>, which is a scripted demonstration and labelled as one.</p>'}, {'q': 'What does workflow automation cost?', 'a': '<p>Two parts: a monthly service subscription that covers hosting, monitoring and support, and a one-off design and build quoted by complexity. Both are confirmed in writing with your quote before anything is built. The honest answer to &ldquo;how much?&rdquo; is that it depends on the number of systems, the volume and the exception handling &mdash; which is why we quote from your actual workflow rather than publishing a number that would be wrong in both directions.</p>'}, {'q': 'How long does a build take?', 'a': '<p>It depends on scope and, honestly, on how quickly we can get access to the systems involved. A simple two-system workflow is a far smaller job than a multi-step orchestration with approvals and reporting. Timescales are confirmed alongside your quote, and we build in stages, so you see the first working step early rather than waiting for a big reveal.</p>'}, {'q': 'Where does our data go?', 'a': '<p>Through the systems in the workflow and nowhere else. We design each workflow to touch only the data it needs, we tell you exactly which systems see what &mdash; including any AI steps &mdash; and data protection is part of the design conversation from the start, not a bolt-on. We are a family-run Dorset business, not an anonymous platform, so you always know who is accountable.</p>'}], 'final_cta': {'h2': 'Ready to stop doing it by hand?', 'lede': 'Describe the repetitive work and we&rsquo;ll map what deterministic automation and AI can honestly take off your team&rsquo;s plate.'}, 'pricing': {'subscription_line': 'Monthly service — confirmed with your quote', 'build_line': 'One-off design and build — quoted by complexity', 'note': 'The monthly service keeps your workflow hosted, monitored and supported by the same local team that looks after your IT. The build is quoted in writing, from your actual workflow, before anything is built.'}, 'service_desc': 'Workflow and business-process automation connecting the systems a business already uses, with AI steps only where they help and human approval on consequential actions.'},
  {'slug': 'ai/agents', 'name': 'AI Agents', 'title': 'Custom AI Agents, Built Around Your Systems | 365 Techies', 'desc': 'Custom AI agents that take bounded, approved actions in your real systems &mdash; built and supported by a family-run Dorset IT team, trading since 1995.', 'eyebrow': 'AI & Automation', 'h1': 'AI agents that do real work, within limits you set', 'hero_lede': 'An agent reads, decides and acts inside the systems you already use &mdash; and asks a human before anything consequential happens. Built and supported by the same Bournemouth team that&rsquo;s looked after local IT since 1995.', 'chips': ['Trading since 1995', '4.9-star Google rating', 'Human approval built in'], 'cta_primary': {'label': "Tell us what's wasting your team's time", 'href': '/ai/start/'}, 'cta_secondary': {'label': 'Try the demo', 'href': '/365-ai-os/'}, 'sections': [{'h2': 'The jobs that quietly eat the week', 'html': '<p>Every business has them. Enquiries that sit in an inbox until someone has a spare minute. Details typed into one system, then retyped into another. Replies that start the same way every single time. None of it is difficult &mdash; it&rsquo;s just constant, and it lands on whoever happens to be nearest.</p><p>An AI agent is a practical way to hand that work over. Not a chatbot bolted onto your website, and not a promise to &ldquo;transform&rdquo; anything &mdash; a piece of software, built around the systems you already use, that does the repetitive middle steps of a job and brings a human in at the moments that matter. This page explains what that means in plain terms, walks through a worked example, and is honest about the limits. If you&rsquo;d rather start with the bigger picture, our <a href="/ai/">AI services overview</a> covers everything we build.</p>'}, {'h2': 'What we mean by a bounded agent', 'html': '<p>&ldquo;Agent&rdquo; has become one of those words that means whatever the person selling it wants it to mean, so here is our definition. An agent is software that can <strong>read</strong> information &mdash; an email, a form, a record &mdash; <strong>decide</strong> what it means using AI, and then <strong>act</strong> on it: draft a reply, update a record, schedule a message, book a slot.</p><p><strong>Bounded</strong> means the acting part is deliberately limited. Every agent we build works from a fixed list of allowed actions, in named systems, under rules you set. It cannot invent new abilities, touch anything outside its brief, or send anything consequential without approval. If a job falls outside its list, it stops and hands over to a person &mdash; it never improvises.</p><p>That constraint isn&rsquo;t a limitation to apologise for. It&rsquo;s the reason the whole thing is trustworthy enough to put anywhere near your customers.</p>'}, {'h2': 'An agent is not a chatbot', 'html': '<p>A chatbot answers questions &mdash; someone asks about opening hours, it replies, and that&rsquo;s the whole job. An agent completes tasks. It might never talk to anyone: it can sit behind a shared inbox reading, sorting, drafting and updating records all day without a chat window in sight.</p><p>The two can work together &mdash; a website assistant that answers questions and then actually books the appointment is a chatbot front with an agent behind it. But you don&rsquo;t need to pick a technology, and we&rsquo;d rather you didn&rsquo;t try. Describe the job in ordinary words &mdash; &ldquo;quotes take too long to go out&rdquo;, &ldquo;nobody updates the spreadsheet&rdquo; &mdash; and we&rsquo;ll tell you which parts suit an agent, which suit ordinary automation, and which should stay with a human.</p>'}, {'h2': 'A worked example, step by step', 'html': '<p>Here is a workflow we build variations of, shown honestly, approval gate included:</p><ul><li><strong>An enquiry arrives</strong> &mdash; a web form or email, at 9pm on a Tuesday, long after everyone has gone home.</li><li><strong>The agent reads it</strong> and extracts the useful details: who it&rsquo;s from, how to reach them, what they&rsquo;re asking for and how urgent it sounds &mdash; even when the message is three rambling paragraphs.</li><li><strong>It drafts a reply</strong> in your tone, answering what can be answered and asking for whatever is missing.</li><li><strong>Nothing is sent.</strong> The draft waits for approval &mdash; a one-tap yes, or a quick edit, from a phone over morning coffee.</li><li><strong>On approval</strong>, the reply goes out, the enquiry is logged in your CRM or job sheet with every detail captured, and the right person is notified to follow up.</li></ul><p>Notice where AI actually sits in that chain: reading a messy email and drafting a decent reply &mdash; the steps that need judgement. Everything else &mdash; sending, logging, notifying &mdash; is deterministic automation, meaning the same input produces the same result every time. We use AI only for the steps where it genuinely helps and reliable, boring automation for the rest, and we&rsquo;ll always tell you which is which.</p>'}, {'h2': 'The systems agents plug into', 'html': '<p>Agents are built around what you already run &mdash; we don&rsquo;t start by asking you to change software. Typical connections include:</p><ul><li>Email and shared inboxes &mdash; reading, sorting, drafting</li><li>Website forms and enquiry pages</li><li>Calendars and booking systems</li><li>CRMs, job sheets and customer records</li><li>Spreadsheets and simple databases</li><li>Messaging &mdash; Slack, Teams and SMS &mdash; for notifications and approvals</li></ul><p>Where a system has a proper integration, we use it. Where it doesn&rsquo;t, there is usually a sensible route in &mdash; a monitored inbox, an export, a form. Occasionally the honest answer is that a system can&rsquo;t be connected safely, in which case we say so before you spend anything. And these aren&rsquo;t hypothetical connections: booking automation, review requests, SMS scheduling and Slack-connected workflows all run in our own business every day, built and maintained in-house.</p>'}, {'h2': 'Limits, approvals and the paper trail', 'html': '<p>Human control isn&rsquo;t a feature we bolt on if you ask for it &mdash; it&rsquo;s how every agent is designed from the first conversation. Each one comes with:</p><ul><li><strong>An allowed-actions list</strong> &mdash; the complete set of things it can do, agreed with you before anything is built.</li><li><strong>Approval gates</strong> on consequential steps &mdash; anything sent to a customer, and anything that commits money or time, waits for a human yes.</li><li><strong>A log</strong> of what it did, when, and what it was acting on, so its work can be checked.</li><li><strong>A pause switch</strong> &mdash; the whole agent can be stopped at any time without breaking anything else.</li></ul><p>You choose where the gates sit. Some businesses keep approval on every outgoing message; others let routine steps run on their own and review only the exceptions. Either is fine, and it can be adjusted as your confidence grows &mdash; that&rsquo;s a conversation, not a rebuild.</p>'}, {'h2': 'Proof you can look at today', 'html': '<p>We&rsquo;ll be straight with you: AI agents are new enough that we don&rsquo;t yet have years of customer case studies to point at, and we won&rsquo;t invent any. What we can show you is working software.</p><p>The <a href="/365-ai-os/">365 AI OS</a> is our Claude-powered AI desktop prototype &mdash; a live, honestly-labelled scripted demonstration of an agent carrying out multi-step work, which you can watch in your browser right now. Our custom monitoring dashboards and the 365 PC Manager app are real products we built and run, and the automations described above &mdash; bookings, review requests, SMS, Slack workflows &mdash; handle our own daily operations. We use this technology on ourselves first.</p><p>Behind it is a family-run firm trading since 1995 with a 4.9-star Google rating, a Microsoft Partner, recognised as a Victron Recommended Software Integrator for our telemetry integration work. If your agent misbehaves at 9am on a Monday, you phone the same local people who look after your IT &mdash; not a ticket queue in another timezone.</p>'}, {'h2': 'What shapes the quote', 'html': '<p>Every agent is priced the same way: a monthly service subscription that keeps it running, monitored and supported, plus a one-off design and build quoted by complexity. There are no tiers to decode, and no figure on this page we&rsquo;d quietly change later &mdash; the build price depends on the job, so we confirm it with your quote.</p><p>What moves the number:</p><ul><li>How many systems the agent connects to, and whether ready-made integrations exist</li><li>How many distinct actions it performs</li><li>Where the approval gates sit, and how many people are involved in them</li><li>Volume &mdash; ten enquiries a week is a different build from two hundred a day</li><li>How sensitive the data is, and what handling that demands</li></ul><p>The first conversation doesn&rsquo;t require you to know any of the vocabulary on this page. <a href="/contact/">Tell us what&rsquo;s wasting your team&rsquo;s time</a> and we&rsquo;ll come back with what an agent could take off it &mdash; and, just as honestly, what it shouldn&rsquo;t.</p>'}], 'faqs': [{'q': 'Will an AI agent email my customers without anyone checking?', 'a': '<p>Not unless you decide it should. By default, anything customer-facing waits for human approval &mdash; the agent drafts, a person taps yes or edits first. Some businesses later let routine, low-stakes messages go out automatically and review only the exceptions, but that is your call to loosen, never our default.</p>'}, {'q': 'What happens when the agent gets something wrong?', 'a': '<p>Because agents are bounded, a mistake is contained: the worst a drafting error can do is produce a bad draft, which the approval step catches. Every action is logged, so we can see exactly what happened and adjust the agent&rsquo;s instructions. And because it&rsquo;s supported by the same local team that looks after your IT, reporting a problem is a phone call, not a ticket into the void.</p>'}, {'q': 'Do we have to change the software we already use?', 'a': '<p>No &mdash; building around your systems is the whole point. Agents connect to the inbox, calendar, CRM and spreadsheets you already run. If a tool genuinely can&rsquo;t be connected safely, we&rsquo;ll tell you before any work is quoted, not after.</p>'}, {'q': 'How is this different from just using ChatGPT?', 'a': '<p>A chat window can draft text, but you do all the carrying &mdash; copying details in, copying answers out, updating your systems yourself, every time. An agent is wired into those systems, follows your agreed process the same way on the hundredth run as the first, and takes actions within set limits, with approvals where they matter. A chat tool is something a person must operate; an agent quietly runs the job.</p>'}, {'q': 'Is our business too small for an AI agent?', 'a': '<p>Size isn&rsquo;t the test &mdash; repetition is. If a task follows a similar pattern many times a week and eats someone&rsquo;s time, it may be worth automating whether you employ two people or forty. We&rsquo;re a family-run small business ourselves, and the automations we run daily were built for a team our size. We&rsquo;ll also tell you honestly when a job is too small or too irregular to justify a build.</p>'}, {'q': 'What about our customer data?', 'a': '<p>Data handling is agreed as part of scoping, not bolted on afterwards. We define exactly what the agent is allowed to read, keep information in your existing systems wherever possible, and avoid moving anything it doesn&rsquo;t need. Data protection obligations are discussed openly at the quote stage, so you know precisely what goes where before anything is built.</p>'}], 'final_cta': {'h2': 'See what a bounded agent could do for you', 'lede': 'Tell us about the process, the systems and where a person must stay in charge &mdash; we&rsquo;ll design the agent around that.'}, 'pricing': {'subscription_line': 'Monthly service — confirmed with your quote', 'build_line': 'One-off design and build — quoted by complexity', 'note': 'Every agent runs as a supported monthly service. The design and build is a separate one-off cost, quoted by complexity once we understand the job — no tiers, and no figure on this page we would quietly change later.'}, 'service_desc': "Custom AI agents that take bounded, approved actions across a business's existing systems - built, secured and supported by a local IT team."},
  {'slug': 'ai/voice-agents', 'name': 'AI Voice Agents', 'title': 'AI Voice Agents from £95/month | 365 Techies', 'desc': 'An AI voice agent that answers after-hours and overflow calls, captures enquiries and books callbacks. From £95/month, supported by our Dorset team.', 'eyebrow': 'AI & Automation', 'h1': 'AI voice agents that answer every call', 'hero_lede': 'An AI voice agent answers your after-hours and overflow calls, captures the enquiry and feeds it straight into your systems &mdash; built, monitored and supported by a family-run Dorset IT team.', 'chips': ['From £95/month', 'Family-run since 1995', '4.9-star Google rating'], 'cta_primary': {'label': "Tell us what's wasting your team's time", 'href': '/ai/start/'}, 'cta_secondary': {'label': 'Explore automations', 'href': '/ai/'}, 'sections': [{'h2': 'The calls you never hear about', 'html': '<p>Most small businesses lose enquiries the same way. The phone rings at 6.40pm and goes to voicemail. It rings at 11am while everyone is with a customer, and the caller waits four rings, hangs up and dials the next company on the list. Very few people leave a voicemail any more &mdash; and fewer still wait for a call back.</p><p>You rarely see this happen, because a missed call leaves almost no trace. There is no enquiry form in your inbox, no note in your diary, no name to ring back. Just a number in a call log that nobody checks.</p><p>An AI voice agent closes that gap. It answers when you can&rsquo;t &mdash; out of hours, on overflow, or whenever you route calls to it &mdash; has a natural conversation with the caller, and makes sure the enquiry ends up somewhere your team will actually see it.</p>'}, {'h2': 'A connected enquiry workflow, not just a receptionist', 'html': '<p>Plenty of services will answer your phone. The difference here is what happens after the call ends.</p><p>We design your voice agent as a complete enquiry workflow. The conversation is only the first half; the second half is deterministic automation &mdash; ordinary, reliable software logic &mdash; that logs the enquiry, raises a ticket or CRM record, and notifies the right person by SMS, email or Slack. AI does the one step where it genuinely helps: understanding a caller who speaks naturally. Everything downstream runs on fixed rules, because a follow-up should never depend on an AI making a judgement call.</p><p>That split matters. It means the agent can be flexible in conversation but completely predictable in what it does with the information &mdash; every call handled the same way, every enquiry landing in the same place. It&rsquo;s the same approach we use across our <a href="/ai/agents/">AI agents</a> work: AI where it helps, plain automation everywhere else.</p>'}, {'h2': 'What the voice agent handles', 'html': '<p>Each agent is designed around your business, but the building blocks are consistent:</p><ul><li><strong>After-hours and overflow answering</strong> &mdash; calls route to the agent when you&rsquo;re closed, engaged or simply busy, on rules you choose.</li><li><strong>Enquiry capture</strong> &mdash; the caller&rsquo;s name, number and what they need, taken in a natural conversation rather than a rigid menu.</li><li><strong>Answering agreed questions</strong> &mdash; opening hours, coverage area, how your service works &mdash; drawn only from information you have approved. The agent doesn&rsquo;t improvise answers.</li><li><strong>Booking and callback capture</strong> &mdash; the agent can take a booking request or arrange a callback slot, so the caller leaves with a next step rather than a promise.</li><li><strong>Message taking</strong> &mdash; a clear, structured message instead of a mumbled voicemail.</li><li><strong>Human transfer</strong> &mdash; during staffed hours, callers who need a person are put through to one. The agent is an extra layer, not a wall.</li><li><strong>The follow-up workflow</strong> &mdash; every call generates a record in your CRM or ticketing system and a notification to your team, automatically.</li></ul>'}, {'h2': 'How a call flows, step by step', 'html': '<p>Here is a typical flow &mdash; yours is designed with you, but this is the shape of it:</p><ul><li><strong>1. The call routes to the agent</strong> &mdash; out of hours, after a set number of rings, or whenever your line is engaged. Your existing number stays the front door.</li><li><strong>2. The caller is told it&rsquo;s an AI</strong> &mdash; a clear, friendly disclosure at the start of every call. We don&rsquo;t build agents that pretend to be human.</li><li><strong>3. The agent asks what they need</strong> &mdash; and the caller answers in their own words. No &ldquo;press one for sales&rdquo;.</li><li><strong>4. It answers or captures</strong> &mdash; agreed questions get answered from your approved information; anything else becomes a structured enquiry with name, number and details.</li><li><strong>5. It offers a next step</strong> &mdash; a booking request, a callback slot, or a transfer to your team if someone is available and the caller needs a person now.</li><li><strong>6. The call ends and is recorded to your agreed setup</strong> &mdash; recording and transcription are configured with you, with retention you have signed off, not defaults you never saw.</li><li><strong>7. The follow-up fires</strong> &mdash; the enquiry is written into your CRM or raised as a ticket, and your team is notified by SMS, email or Slack. By the time you pick up your phone, the enquiry is already in your system with a record of what was said.</li></ul><p>Nothing in steps six and seven involves AI judgement &mdash; it&rsquo;s fixed automation, which is exactly why it&rsquo;s dependable.</p>'}, {'h2': 'Honest about the details', 'html': '<p>A few things other providers tend to gloss over, stated plainly:</p><ul><li><strong>Callers always know.</strong> The agent discloses that it&rsquo;s an AI assistant at the start of every call. In our experience people are fine with that &mdash; what frustrates callers is not being answered at all.</li><li><strong>Recording and data are agreed, not assumed.</strong> Running the service means calls are processed, and usually recorded and transcribed, by the underlying telephony and AI platforms. We document exactly what is stored, where, and for how long, and we set retention with you &mdash; the setup is designed to support your obligations under UK data protection law, and you&rsquo;ll see it in writing before anything goes live.</li><li><strong>Usage costs are itemised.</strong> Call minutes and telephony charges depend on your call volume, so we identify them line by line in your quote rather than burying them in the subscription or promising they&rsquo;re &ldquo;included forever&rdquo;.</li><li><strong>Humans stay in control.</strong> The agent captures and routes; it doesn&rsquo;t commit your business to anything. Anything consequential goes to a person, and transfer to your team is part of the design, not an afterthought.</li></ul>'}, {'h2': 'Built, monitored and supported in Dorset', 'html': '<p>We&rsquo;re a family-run IT support business in Bournemouth, trading since 1995, and your voice agent is looked after by the same local team that can look after the rest of your IT. It isn&rsquo;t a portal login and a chatbot for support &mdash; it&rsquo;s people you can ring, serving Bournemouth, Poole, Christchurch and the wider Dorset area, and remotely across the UK where that suits.</p><p>We also hold ourselves to the standard we&rsquo;d ask of anyone else: proof before promises. There are no AI customer case studies on this page because we won&rsquo;t invent them &mdash; instead, we can show you the systems we run on ourselves. Our own booking automation, review-request automation, SMS scheduling and Slack-connected workflows were all built in-house and handle real work in our business every day. You can also see our approach to AI in the open on the <a href="/365-ai-os/">365 AI OS demonstration</a> &mdash; a scripted demo, honestly labelled as one.</p><p>The monthly subscription is a service, not just a licence: we monitor the agent, review how calls are being handled, adjust the call flows as your business changes, and stay accountable for the result.</p>'}, {'h2': 'What it costs', 'html': '<p>The pricing follows the same model as every service on our <a href="/ai/">AI &amp; automation</a> pages: a monthly service subscription plus a one-off design and build.</p><ul><li><strong>Service subscription &mdash; from &pound;95/month.</strong> This covers running the agent, monitoring, support from our team, and ongoing adjustments to what it says and where enquiries go.</li><li><strong>Design and build &mdash; one-off, quoted by complexity.</strong> A simple after-hours answering and message flow sits at one end; an agent wired into your CRM, booking system and ticketing sits at the other. The quote reflects the number of call flows, the questions you want answered, and the systems we connect.</li><li><strong>Telephony and usage &mdash; identified in your quote.</strong> Call minutes vary by business, so we show them separately and honestly.</li></ul><p>To get a quote, we start with a short conversation about your calls &mdash; when they&rsquo;re missed, what callers usually want, and where enquiries should land. <a href="/contact/">Tell us what&rsquo;s wasting your team&rsquo;s time</a> and we&rsquo;ll take it from there.</p>'}], 'faqs': [{'q': 'How much does an AI voice agent cost?', 'a': '<p>The service subscription starts from &pound;95/month, which covers running the agent plus monitoring, support and ongoing adjustments by our team. The design and build is a separate one-off cost, quoted by complexity &mdash; a simple message-taking flow costs less to build than an agent connected to your CRM and booking system. Call minutes and telephony charges are identified separately in your quote, so you see the full running cost up front.</p>'}, {'q': 'Will callers know they are talking to an AI?', 'a': '<p>Yes, always. The agent discloses at the start of every call that it&rsquo;s an AI assistant. We don&rsquo;t build agents that pretend to be human &mdash; and in practice, callers care far more about being answered and helped than about who answered.</p>'}, {'q': "What happens if the agent can't help a caller?", 'a': '<p>During staffed hours it transfers the caller to your team. Out of hours it takes a structured message, offers a callback slot, and notifies you immediately &mdash; so the caller always leaves with a next step, and you always know the call happened. The agent only answers questions from information you have approved; it never guesses.</p>'}, {'q': 'Will this replace our receptionist?', 'a': '<p>That&rsquo;s not how we design it. The agent handles the calls a person can&rsquo;t &mdash; after hours, when the line is engaged, when everyone is with a customer. If you have someone answering the phone, they keep doing what they do well, and the agent stops enquiries falling through the gaps around them.</p>'}, {'q': 'Do we need a new phone number or new lines?', 'a': '<p>Usually not. Your existing number stays the front door, and calls divert to the agent on rules you choose &mdash; out of hours, after a set number of rings, or on overflow. We handle the telephony setup as part of the build, and any line or call costs are identified in your quote.</p>'}, {'q': 'What happens to call recordings and caller data?', 'a': '<p>We tell you exactly what is stored, where, and for how long, before anything goes live. Recording and transcription settings and retention periods are agreed with you, and the setup is designed to support your obligations under UK data protection law. Every enquiry the agent captures lands in your systems &mdash; your CRM, your ticketing, your notifications &mdash; not in a black box.</p>'}], 'final_cta': {'h2': 'Stop missing calls', 'lede': 'An AI voice agent that answers every call, captures every enquiry and books the work &mdash; from &pound;95 a month, with the design and build quoted by complexity.'}, 'pricing': {'subscription_line': 'From £95/month — monthly service subscription', 'build_line': 'One-off design and build — quoted by complexity', 'note': 'Call minutes, telephony and any usage charges are identified line by line in your quote, so you can see the full running cost before you commit.'}, 'service_desc': 'AI voice receptionist and phone-answering agents that answer calls, capture enquiries and book callbacks 24/7 for small businesses.'},
  {'slug': 'ai/consultancy', 'name': 'AI Consultancy', 'title': 'AI Consultancy Bournemouth & Dorset | 365 Techies', 'desc': 'Not sure where to start with AI? We map your processes, measure a baseline and tell you honestly what is worth automating. Advice from a Dorset IT team.', 'eyebrow': 'AI & Automation', 'h1': 'AI consultancy for businesses that aren&rsquo;t sure where to start', 'hero_lede': 'Before anyone talks technology, we sit with your team, map how the work actually happens and measure what it costs you. Then we tell you &mdash; honestly &mdash; what&rsquo;s worth automating, what isn&rsquo;t, and what it would take.', 'chips': ['Trading since 1995', '4.9-star Google rating', 'Family-run in Bournemouth'], 'cta_primary': {'label': "Tell us what's wasting your team's time", 'href': '/ai/start/'}, 'cta_secondary': {'label': 'See how it works', 'href': '/365-ai-os/'}, 'sections': [{'h2': 'The signs a process is worth a look', 'html': '<p>You don&rsquo;t need to arrive with a shortlist of technologies, and you certainly don&rsquo;t need to know what a large language model is. The useful starting point is much simpler: where does your team&rsquo;s week actually go? In most of the businesses we look after, the candidates announce themselves:</p><ul><li><strong>Repeated copying.</strong> Someone re-types the same details from an email into a spreadsheet, then into an invoice, then into a job sheet &mdash; several times a day, every day.</li><li><strong>Chasing.</strong> Quotes that need a follow-up, invoices that need a nudge, customers who said &ldquo;call me next week&rdquo; &mdash; all held in one person&rsquo;s head or on a to-do list that never empties.</li><li><strong>Missed enquiries.</strong> Calls and web enquiries that land at 6pm on a Friday and wait until Monday, by which point the customer has phoned someone else.</li><li><strong>Spreadsheet glue.</strong> A spreadsheet that quietly holds two systems together, maintained by the one person who understands it &mdash; and who occasionally goes on holiday.</li><li><strong>The same questions, again.</strong> Opening hours, prices, booking steps, &ldquo;where are we with my job&rdquo; &mdash; answered by hand, dozens of times a week.</li></ul><p>If any of those sound familiar, there&rsquo;s probably something worth examining. Whether the fix involves AI at all is a separate question &mdash; and one we answer with evidence, not enthusiasm.</p>'}, {'h2': 'How discovery works', 'html': '<p>Consultancy with us is a structured piece of work, not a sales meeting with slides. It&rsquo;s the same method we used to automate our own business, and it runs in a fixed order because each step depends on the one before it:</p><ul><li><strong>Process interviews.</strong> We sit with the people who actually do the work &mdash; not just the owner &mdash; and walk through a real week. The exceptions and workarounds they mention in passing are usually where the time goes.</li><li><strong>Workflow mapping.</strong> We draw each process as it really runs: every handover, every wait, every &ldquo;then I copy it into the other system&rdquo;. You see the map and correct it before we go any further.</li><li><strong>Systems and data inventory.</strong> We list the software you use, where your data lives, and which systems have an API &mdash; the plumbing that lets one system talk to another. This decides what can be connected cheaply and what can&rsquo;t.</li><li><strong>Baseline measurement.</strong> We count and time the work as it stands today: how many enquiries a week, how long a quote takes, how often invoices get chased. Real numbers, taken from your systems and your people.</li><li><strong>Risk and approval mapping.</strong> For each candidate process we agree what a human must check before anything is sent, changed or paid. Consequential actions get an approval step designed in from the start; routine ones don&rsquo;t need one.</li><li><strong>Prioritised opportunity list.</strong> Everything we find, ranked by measured time against build effort &mdash; with a plain-English solution outline for the items worth doing first.</li></ul>'}, {'h2': 'Why we measure before we promise', 'html': '<p>A lot of AI marketing leads with a percentage: hours saved, costs cut, productivity up. We don&rsquo;t, because without a baseline those numbers are fiction. If nobody has measured how long your invoicing run takes today, any claim about how much faster it could be is a guess dressed up as a promise.</p><p>So we measure first. The baseline does three jobs: it tells us whether a process is worth automating at all, it lets us rank opportunities on evidence rather than novelty, and &mdash; most importantly &mdash; it gives you something to hold us to. If we go on to build a system, you can compare the numbers after go-live with the numbers before, in your own figures, and see whether it earned its keep.</p><p>It&rsquo;s the standard we apply to ourselves. The booking automation, review requests and SMS scheduling that run inside 365 Techies every day were built the same way &mdash; look at the numbers first, build second, then check the numbers again.</p>'}, {'h2': 'What you get at the end', 'html': '<p>Discovery finishes with something you can hold, read and act on &mdash; whether or not you act on it with us:</p><ul><li>A <strong>map of each process</strong> we examined, drawn as it actually runs today.</li><li>Your <strong>baseline figures</strong> &mdash; the counts and timings that describe what each process currently costs you.</li><li>A <strong>prioritised opportunity list</strong>, with every item honestly labelled: a job for AI, a job for rules-based automation, a process change, or leave it alone.</li><li>A <strong>solution outline</strong> for the top items &mdash; what the system would do, which of your existing tools it connects to, and exactly where a human approves before anything consequential happens.</li><li>A <strong>quote</strong> &mdash; a one-off design and build figure based on complexity, plus the monthly service subscription that keeps the system monitored, maintained and supported once it&rsquo;s live.</li></ul><p>From there the decision is yours: start with the top item, hand us the whole list, or decide the honest answer is &ldquo;not yet&rdquo; &mdash; the map and the numbers are still yours to keep. And if you already know which process you want examined, you can <a href="/contact/">request an AI quote</a> and go straight to scoping.</p>'}, {'h2': 'Sometimes the answer isn&rsquo;t AI', 'html': '<p>This is the part of the conversation most AI companies skip. A good share of the problems we&rsquo;re shown don&rsquo;t need AI at all &mdash; they need <strong>deterministic automation</strong>: rules-based systems where the same input always produces the same output. If a booking should always create a calendar entry, send a confirmation and add the job to a list, that&rsquo;s a rule, not a judgement. Rules are cheaper to build, simple to test, and they never have an off day.</p><p>AI earns its place where a step needs judgement over messy input &mdash; reading an enquiry and drafting a sensible reply, summarising a long email thread, answering a phone call in natural language. In the systems we design, AI does that one step and deterministic automation does everything around it. Most of the working systems on our <a href="/ai/automations/">automation page</a> are exactly this shape, and we&rsquo;ll always tell you which parts are which.</p><p>Sometimes the answer is a process change and no software at all. If discovery shows a form asks for information nobody ever uses, the recommendation is to delete the field &mdash; not to build a robot to fill it in. You&rsquo;ll get that advice just as readily, because advice you can trust is the entire point of asking for it.</p>'}, {'h2': 'Advice from people who run these systems every day', 'html': '<p>There&rsquo;s no shortage of AI consultants at the moment. What&rsquo;s rarer is one who can show you the things they&rsquo;re describing actually running in their own business. We can. The booking automation, review-request emails, SMS scheduling and Slack-connected workflows we recommend are the same ones running inside 365 Techies today &mdash; built in-house, in production, doing real work. Our <a href="/365-ai-os/">365 AI OS demo</a> shows a Claude-powered AI desktop we built ourselves (a scripted demonstration, and clearly labelled as one), and our monitoring dashboards run live on this site.</p><p>We&rsquo;re not new to unglamorous, reliable systems either. We&rsquo;ve been a family-run IT support business in Bournemouth since 1995, we hold a 4.9-star Google rating, we&rsquo;re a Microsoft Partner, and our telemetry work earned Victron Energy&rsquo;s Recommended Software Integrator status. Whatever comes out of your discovery is supported by the same local team that looks after your IT &mdash; across Bournemouth, Poole, Christchurch and the wider Dorset area, and remotely across the UK where that suits. If you&rsquo;d like to see what the builds themselves look like, start with our <a href="/ai/">AI services overview</a>.</p>'}], 'faqs': [{'q': 'We don&rsquo;t know anything about AI. Is that a problem?', 'a': '<p>No &mdash; it&rsquo;s the normal starting point, and it&rsquo;s why this service exists. You never need to pick a technology: you describe the work, we map it, and we come back with recommendations in plain English. Where a term matters &mdash; an API, a large language model, deterministic automation &mdash; we explain it the first time we use it. The decisions you&rsquo;ll actually be asked to make are business decisions: which process matters most, what a human must approve, and whether the quote stacks up against the measured time that process costs you.</p>'}, {'q': 'How long does discovery take?', 'a': '<p>It depends on scope, which is why it&rsquo;s quoted rather than sold off a rate card. Looking at one process &mdash; say, how enquiries are handled from first contact to booked job &mdash; is a much smaller piece of work than mapping a whole business. When we scope the work we&rsquo;ll tell you how many interviews we need, whose time we&rsquo;ll take and for how long, and when you&rsquo;ll have the written findings. Most of it fits around your team&rsquo;s normal day; nobody has to down tools for a week.</p>'}, {'q': 'What does AI consultancy cost?', 'a': '<p>Consultancy is scoped and quoted before any work starts, based on how many processes and systems we&rsquo;re examining &mdash; so you&rsquo;ll know the figure up front. If the findings lead to a build, that&rsquo;s quoted separately by complexity, and the finished system then runs as a monthly service subscription covering monitoring, maintenance and support. There are no tiers to decode and no obligation to build anything: the findings and the baseline figures are yours either way.</p>'}, {'q': 'Won&rsquo;t you just recommend AI because AI is what you sell?', 'a': '<p>No &mdash; and the written findings make that hard to fudge, because every opportunity is labelled as AI, rules-based automation, a process change, or not worth doing. In our own business a large share of the wins turned out to be plain rules-based automation: cheaper to build, simpler to test, with AI reserved for the steps that need judgement over messy input. We make our living from ongoing service on systems that keep working, so recommending the wrong tool would cost us more than it earned.</p>'}, {'q': 'What happens to our data during discovery?', 'a': '<p>Discovery is mostly conversation and observation &mdash; we map how work flows and where data lives, and we don&rsquo;t need copies of your customer records to do that. We never ask for your passwords. Where a recommended system would handle personal data, the solution outline states exactly what it touches, which systems it connects to, and where a human approves before anything is sent or changed. Data protection is treated as a design constraint from the first drawing, not a box ticked at the end.</p>'}, {'q': 'Do you only work with businesses near Bournemouth?', 'a': '<p>We&rsquo;re based in Bournemouth, and most of our clients are across Bournemouth, Poole, Christchurch and the wider Dorset area &mdash; which is where on-site interviews are easiest to arrange. But discovery works well remotely too, with interviews over video call and the workflow maps shared on screen, so we take on work across the UK where that suits. What doesn&rsquo;t change is the accountability: a named, family-run team that&rsquo;s been trading since 1995, and that you can phone.</p>'}], 'final_cta': {'h2': 'Not sure where to start? That&rsquo;s the point of this.', 'lede': 'We&rsquo;ll map the process, measure the baseline and give you a prioritised, honest plan &mdash; including when the answer isn&rsquo;t AI at all.'}, 'pricing': {'subscription_line': 'Monthly service &mdash; confirmed with your quote', 'build_line': 'One-off design &amp; build &mdash; quoted by complexity', 'note': 'Consultancy and discovery work is scoped and quoted before it starts. A monthly service subscription applies where we go on to implement a system for you &mdash; it covers monitoring, maintenance and support from the same local team that looks after your IT.'}, 'service_desc': 'AI strategy and discovery for SMEs - process mapping, systems and data inventory, baseline measurement and a prioritised automation plan.'},
  {'slug': 'ai/training', 'name': 'AI Training', 'title': 'AI Training for Teams in Dorset | 365 Techies', 'desc': 'Role-based AI training for business teams across Dorset. Safe-use policy, approved tools and real follow-up, from a local team that uses AI every day.', 'eyebrow': 'AI & Automation', 'h1': 'AI training built on the work your team actually does', 'hero_lede': 'Practical, role-based sessions that get your team using Copilot, ChatGPT or Claude safely and productively &mdash; taught by a Dorset firm that runs these tools in its own business every day.', 'chips': ['Trading since 1995', '4.9-star Google rating', 'In person across Dorset or remote'], 'cta_primary': {'label': "Tell us what's wasting your team's time", 'href': '/ai/start/'}, 'cta_secondary': {'label': 'Try the demo', 'href': '/365-ai-os/'}, 'sections': [{'h2': 'Who this training is for', 'html': '<p>This page is for owners and managers who want a whole team using AI properly &mdash; not one enthusiast quietly experimenting while everyone else waits to be told what&rsquo;s allowed. Perhaps you&rsquo;re weighing up Microsoft Copilot licences. Perhaps half your staff already paste things into ChatGPT and you&rsquo;re not entirely sure what. Either way, the questions are the same: which tools, for which jobs, with what rules?</p><p>We train teams across Bournemouth, Poole, Christchurch and the wider Dorset area &mdash; and remotely across the UK where that suits better &mdash; on Copilot, ChatGPT and Claude, using your own workflows as the course material. If you&rsquo;re an individual who just wants a friendly starting point, our free <a href="/ai-for-beginners-course/">AI for Beginners course</a> is the better fit. This page is about scoped, quoted enablement for a team.</p>'}, {'h2': 'Sessions built on your real workflows', 'html': '<p>Generic AI courses teach prompts in the abstract, and the learning evaporates by Friday. We start from the other end: what does your team actually do all day? Quotes and estimates, customer emails, job sheets, meeting notes, reports, spreadsheets, chasing invoices &mdash; every business has a different list, and every role on the team has a different slice of it.</p><p>We build role-based sessions around that list. What your office administrator needs from Copilot is not what your estimator or your director needs, so we don&rsquo;t sit them through the same slides. Each person practises on realistic examples from their own job &mdash; agreed with you in advance, so nothing sensitive ends up on a screen &mdash; and leaves with working patterns they can use the next morning.</p><p>We&rsquo;re also honest about the limits. Some tasks shouldn&rsquo;t be a person prompting a chatbot at all &mdash; they should be a quiet automation running reliably in the background. When training uncovers one of those, we say so; building them is what our <a href="/ai/">wider AI services</a> are for.</p>'}, {'h2': 'Safe use: the habits and the policy', 'html': '<p>Before anyone gets faster with AI, they need to know where the edges are. The most valuable hour of the programme is usually the one about what <strong>never</strong> goes into an AI tool: customer names and personal details, passwords and access keys, card or bank information, unreleased financial figures, anything covered by an NDA.</p><p>There&rsquo;s a plain reason why. Some free, consumer-grade chatbot plans can use what you type to improve their models unless the settings say otherwise. Business-grade tools handle data differently &mdash; which is exactly why choosing the right tier matters. We explain your data-protection responsibilities in plain English, including what UK GDPR means for pasting customer information into a chatbot, without pretending a training day makes compliance somebody else&rsquo;s problem.</p><p>You also leave with a short, readable AI use policy written for your business: which tools are approved, what the data rules are, when to disclose that AI was involved, and the habit that underpins everything &mdash; AI drafts, a person decides. Outputs get checked by a human before they reach a customer, a contract or a set of accounts.</p>'}, {'h2': 'Approved tools, chosen for how you work', 'html': '<p>You shouldn&rsquo;t have to become a technology analyst to choose between Copilot, ChatGPT and Claude &mdash; that&rsquo;s our job. We recommend tools based on what you already pay for, how each one handles your data, and what the actual work is. Often the honest answer is a mix: Copilot where it lives naturally inside Microsoft 365, a different tool where it&rsquo;s simply better at the task in hand.</p><p>As a Microsoft Partner we&rsquo;ll also tell you who on the team would genuinely use a Copilot licence and who wouldn&rsquo;t &mdash; it&rsquo;s a paid add-on per user, and there&rsquo;s no sense paying for seats that will sit idle. The approved-tools list we hand over names specific tools and tiers, so nobody on your team has to guess what&rsquo;s allowed.</p>'}, {'h2': 'After the sessions: adoption, not a certificate', 'html': '<p>Most workplace training fades within a fortnight, which is why we don&rsquo;t sell training days and disappear. The monthly service that sits behind every programme covers the part that actually changes behaviour: check-ins with the team, a simple agreed measure of which workflows have genuinely moved across, refresher sessions when the tools change &mdash; and they change constantly &mdash; and updates to your policy and approved-tools list as new features arrive.</p><p>It also keeps a door open. When follow-up shows a task that&rsquo;s still eating time, sometimes the answer is another session &mdash; and sometimes it&rsquo;s handing the task to an <a href="/ai/agents/">AI agent or automation</a> instead. Because the same team does both, your training feeds implementation rather than stopping at a certificate.</p>'}, {'h2': 'Taught by people who use this every day', 'html': '<p>Plenty of AI training is delivered by people who have never run these tools outside a classroom. We&rsquo;re an IT support firm, family-run and trading since 1995, and we use AI and automation in our own business every day: our booking automation, review requests, SMS scheduling and Slack-connected workflows were all built in-house and are running right now &mdash; for us. When we show your team a workflow, it&rsquo;s one we operate, not one we read about.</p><p>You can see the sharp end of that on our <a href="/365-ai-os/">365 AI OS demo</a> &mdash; a working, Claude-powered AI desktop prototype, honestly labelled as a scripted demonstration. And after the training, your team is supported by the same local people who look after your IT, backed by a 4.9-star Google rating.</p>'}, {'h2': 'How it works', 'html': '<p>No jargon required at any stage &mdash; you describe the work, we bring the technology.</p><ul><li><strong>Tell us about the team.</strong> A conversation about roles, workflows and where the time goes.</li><li><strong>We scope and quote.</strong> A written quote per team: one-off programme design and delivery, quoted by complexity, plus the monthly follow-up service.</li><li><strong>Sessions run.</strong> In person at your premises across Bournemouth, Poole, Christchurch and wider Dorset, or remotely UK-wide.</li><li><strong>Policy and tools handed over.</strong> Your AI use policy and approved-tools list, written for your business.</li><li><strong>Follow-up begins.</strong> Check-ins, measurement and refreshers under the monthly service.</li></ul><p>This isn&rsquo;t hourly tutoring, and it isn&rsquo;t a webinar. Tutors teach one person to prompt; this is enablement scoped to a whole team, tied to the systems that team uses &mdash; and, where it helps, to the automations behind them.</p>'}], 'faqs': [{'q': 'Is Copilot included in our Microsoft 365 subscription?', 'a': '<p>No &mdash; Microsoft 365 Copilot is a separate paid add-on licence per user, on top of a standard business plan. Part of scoping the training is working out who on your team would genuinely use it before you commit to licences. Some roles get real value from it; others do fine with carefully chosen free or lower-cost tools used safely. As a Microsoft Partner we can set the licences up for you if you decide to go ahead.</p>'}, {'q': 'What should our staff never paste into ChatGPT?', 'a': '<p>Customer names and personal details, passwords and access keys, card or bank information, unreleased financial figures, and anything covered by an NDA. Some free chatbot plans can use what you type to improve their models unless the settings say otherwise, so the safe habit is to stop before pasting, not after. The training builds that habit, and your AI use policy writes it down so nobody has to rely on memory.</p>'}, {'q': 'How much does team AI training cost?', 'a': '<p>It&rsquo;s quoted per team. The one-off programme &mdash; design and delivery &mdash; is quoted by complexity: how many roles, how many people, and how much workflow mapping is involved. The ongoing follow-up runs as a monthly service, confirmed in the same quote. There are no tiers or per-seat price lists to decode; <a href="/contact/">tell us about your team</a> and you&rsquo;ll get one written quote covering both.</p>'}, {'q': 'Can you run the sessions at our offices?', 'a': '<p>Yes. We deliver in person at your premises across Bournemouth, Poole, Christchurch and the wider Dorset area, and remotely across the UK where that suits the team better. Many businesses mix the two &mdash; an in-person session to start, remote refreshers afterwards.</p>'}, {'q': 'Half our team already uses ChatGPT. Do we still need training?', 'a': '<p>That&rsquo;s usually the strongest reason to do it. Informal use means every person has invented their own habits &mdash; some good, some risky &mdash; and nobody knows what data has already been pasted where. Training doesn&rsquo;t start people from zero; it levels the team up to a shared standard, closes the data-safety gaps, and usually shows even confident users techniques they&rsquo;d missed.</p>'}, {'q': 'How is this different from your free AI for Beginners course?', 'a': '<p>The <a href="/ai-for-beginners-course/">free beginners course</a> is for individuals who want a gentle, general introduction at their own pace. This training is scoped for a specific team: sessions built on your actual workflows, a written AI use policy, an approved-tools list, and follow-up under a monthly service. If you&rsquo;re one person exploring AI, start with the course; if you&rsquo;re responsible for a team, start here.</p>'}], 'final_cta': {'h2': 'Give your team confident, safe AI habits', 'lede': 'Practical sessions built on your real workflows &mdash; what to use, what never to paste in, and how to make it stick.'}, 'pricing': {'subscription_line': 'Monthly service &mdash; confirmed with your quote', 'build_line': 'One-off programme design &amp; delivery &mdash; quoted per team', 'note': 'One conversation about your team, one written quote covering both. No tiers, no per-seat price lists.'}, 'service_desc': 'Practical AI training and adoption for business teams - safe, role-based use of Copilot, ChatGPT and Claude built around real workflows.'},
  {'slug': 'ai/tools', 'name': 'AI Tools & Demos', 'title': 'AI Tools & Live Demos | 365 Techies', 'desc': 'Try our free AI ROI estimator, the 365 AI OS prototype demo and a live 3D monitoring dashboard. Built in-house in Bournemouth and honestly labelled.', 'eyebrow': 'AI &amp; Automation', 'h1': 'AI tools and demos you can try today', 'hero_lede': 'Most AI pages ask you to imagine. These are three things you can actually click &mdash; a free estimator, a working prototype and a live dashboard &mdash; all built and run by the same Bournemouth team that would build yours.', 'chips': ['Three tools, free to try', 'Built and run in-house', '4.9-star Google rating'], 'cta_primary': {'label': "Tell us what's wasting your team's time", 'href': '/ai/start/'}, 'cta_secondary': {'label': 'Try the demo', 'href': '/365-ai-os/'}, 'sections': [{'h2': 'Why we publish working demos', 'html': '<p>Plenty of firms will tell you what AI could do for your business. Very few will show you anything actually running. We&rsquo;ve taken the opposite approach: everything on this page is something we built ourselves and use in our own business &mdash; the same team that runs booking, review-request and messaging automations to keep 365 Techies going day to day.</p><p>Nothing here asks for your email address or books you into a sales call. Try the tools, watch the demos, and if something looks useful, <a href="/contact/">tell us what&rsquo;s wasting your team&rsquo;s time</a>.</p>'}, {'h2': 'The AI ROI calculator', 'html': '<p>The <a href="/ai-roi-calculator/">AI ROI calculator</a> is a free estimator. You put in the repetitive jobs your team does &mdash; how often they come up, how long they take, and roughly what an hour of that person&rsquo;s time costs &mdash; and it works out what automating those steps might be worth over a month and a year.</p><p><strong>What it is:</strong> honest arithmetic based on the numbers you type in. <strong>What it isn&rsquo;t:</strong> a promise. The results are estimates only, not customer outcomes, and we&rsquo;ll always confirm what&rsquo;s realistic for your business before quoting for a build. Nothing you enter is stored unless you choose to send it to us.</p>'}, {'h2': 'The 365 AI OS demo', 'html': '<p>The <a href="/365-ai-os/">365 AI OS</a> is our working prototype of a Claude-powered AI desktop &mdash; an assistant that can see what&rsquo;s on screen and work across applications rather than sitting in a single chat box. The demo on that page is a scripted demonstration, and it says so plainly: it walks through a short scripted sequence so you can see how the prototype behaves without waiting for anything to load or go wrong.</p><p>It&rsquo;s free to watch, there&rsquo;s no sign-up, and because it&rsquo;s a demonstration it never touches your files or your data. If it sparks ideas, our <a href="/ai/agents/">AI agents</a> page explains how the same thinking applies to everyday business jobs.</p>'}, {'h2': 'The live 3D system-monitoring demo', 'html': '<p>The <a href="/system-monitoring-demo/">live 3D system-monitoring demo</a> shows our dashboards product doing its actual job: a fully interactive 3D dashboard running on clearly labelled sample data &mdash; exactly how a customer&rsquo;s own version looks and moves. This is the same product we build for customers &mdash; from office PCs through to marine and off-grid energy systems, where we&rsquo;re a Victron Recommended Software Integrator.</p><p>The demo is view-only. It runs on sample data &mdash; nothing connects to your systems, and the page says so, and it neither asks for nor stores anything of yours.</p>'}, {'h2': 'Where these tools lead', 'html': '<p>These tools exist to make the next conversation easier, not to replace it. If the calculator surfaces a job worth automating, the <a href="/ai/">AI services section</a> shows how we&rsquo;d approach it, and the <a href="/ai/agents/">AI agents</a> page covers work that needs judgement as well as repetition.</p><p>Two things stay true across everything we build. First, AI only does a step where it genuinely helps; ordinary rule-based automation handles the rest, and we&rsquo;ll tell you which is which. Second, a human can stay in the loop &mdash; consequential actions can wait for your approval rather than happening silently.</p><p>Every service works the same way commercially: a monthly service subscription plus a one-off design and build, quoted by complexity once we understand the job. No tiers to decode and no technology for you to pick &mdash; just <a href="/contact/">tell us what you want to automate</a> and we&rsquo;ll come back with a plain-English plan and a price.</p>'}], 'faqs': [{'q': 'Are these tools really free to use?', 'a': '<p>Yes. The calculator, the 365 AI OS demo and the monitoring demo are all free, with no sign-up and no sales call attached. They exist so you can see what we do before deciding whether to talk to us.</p>'}, {'q': 'Is the ROI calculator telling me what I&rsquo;d actually save?', 'a': '<p>No &mdash; it produces estimates from the numbers you enter, not measured customer results. It&rsquo;s a useful way to spot which jobs might be worth automating, but we&rsquo;d always confirm what&rsquo;s realistic for your business before quoting anything.</p>'}, {'q': 'Can I buy the 365 AI OS today?', 'a': '<p>It&rsquo;s a working prototype rather than a boxed product, and the demo is a scripted demonstration &mdash; labelled as such on the page. If the idea interests you, <a href="/contact/">talk to us</a> about what a practical build for your business would look like.</p>'}, {'q': 'Do you store what I type into the calculator?', 'a': '<p>No. Nothing you enter is stored unless you choose to send it to us &mdash; for example, by asking for a quote afterwards.</p>'}, {'q': 'What happens if I try a demo and want to go further?', 'a': '<p>Tell us about the job you want off your team&rsquo;s plate. We&rsquo;ll look at it, tell you honestly whether AI, straightforward automation or neither is the right answer, and quote a monthly service plus a one-off build priced by complexity. We&rsquo;re the same Bournemouth-based, family-run team that&rsquo;s looked after local IT since 1995, so you&rsquo;ll be dealing with people you can actually phone.</p>'}], 'final_cta': {'h2': 'Try something real', 'lede': 'Run the estimator, walk through the AI OS demo, poke the live dashboard &mdash; then tell us what you&rsquo;d like your version to do.'}, 'pricing': {'subscription_line': 'Monthly service &mdash; confirmed with your quote', 'build_line': 'One-off design and build &mdash; quoted by complexity', 'note': 'Everything on this page is free to use. Subscription and build pricing only applies when you ask us to build something for your business.'}},
  {'slug': 'ai/industries', 'name': 'AI by Industry', 'title': 'AI by Industry for Dorset Businesses | 365 Techies', 'desc': 'How AI and automation fit real workflows in professional services, trades, and manufacturing, marine and energy businesses across Dorset.', 'eyebrow': 'AI &amp; Automation', 'h1': 'AI that fits the way your industry works', 'hero_lede': 'The same automation building blocks land very differently in an accountancy practice, a heating firm and a marine workshop. Here&rsquo;s where they fit the work you actually do &mdash; and where our proof comes from.', 'chips': ['Trading since 1995', '4.9-star Google rating', 'Victron Recommended Integrator'], 'cta_primary': {'label': "Tell us what's wasting your team's time", 'href': '/ai/start/'}, 'cta_secondary': {'label': 'See how it works', 'href': '/365-ai-os/'}, 'sections': [{'h2': 'Start with the workflow, not the technology', 'html': '<p>Every page in our <a href="/ai/">AI services</a> section works the same way: you describe a job that eats your team&rsquo;s time, and we work out which parts a computer should do. Sometimes that means AI &mdash; reading a document, drafting a reply, answering a phone call. Just as often it means plain, deterministic automation: rules that fire the same way every time, with no guesswork involved. We use AI only for the steps where it genuinely helps, and we&rsquo;ll tell you which is which.</p><p>The three lanes below are where the workflows we build show up most often in Dorset businesses. You don&rsquo;t need to pick a technology, and your business doesn&rsquo;t need to match a lane exactly &mdash; the patterns travel well.</p>'}, {'h2': 'Professional and business services', 'html': '<p>Accountancy practices, legal firms, agencies and consultancies run on documents, deadlines and client communication &mdash; and most of the wasted time sits in the gaps between them. Chasing clients for records ahead of a filing deadline. Copying details from an email into a practice management system. Writing the same status update for the tenth time this week.</p><p>Typical systems we connect to: Microsoft 365 and Outlook, practice or case management software, and accounting packages such as Xero, QuickBooks or Sage. What changes when the workflow is automated:</p><ul><li><strong>Document chasing runs itself</strong> &mdash; polite, scheduled reminders that stop the moment the client responds.</li><li><strong>Deadlines drive the diary</strong> &mdash; work is queued and flagged from real dates, not from someone remembering.</li><li><strong>Routine drafts arrive ready for review</strong> &mdash; an <a href="/ai/agents/">AI agent</a> prepares the email or summary; a person approves it before anything is sent.</li></ul><p>Evidence status, honestly: we have no client case studies in this lane yet. What we can show you is the same pattern running in our own business &mdash; automated booking confirmations, review requests and follow-ups that go out every day without anyone touching them.</p>'}, {'h2': 'Property, trades and field services', 'html': '<p>Estate and lettings agents, builders, plumbers, electricians, heating engineers, landscapers &mdash; businesses where the person who wins the work is also up a ladder when the phone rings. The expensive problems are familiar: missed calls that become someone else&rsquo;s customer, quotes that never get a follow-up, job sheets that live on paper until Friday night.</p><p>Typical systems: a mobile phone, WhatsApp, a shared calendar, and often a job management app. What changes:</p><ul><li><strong>Missed calls get answered</strong> &mdash; an <a href="/ai/voice-agents/">AI voice agent</a> takes the caller&rsquo;s details, explains when you&rsquo;ll call back, and can book an appointment straight into your diary.</li><li><strong>Quotes are followed up on a schedule</strong> &mdash; a courteous nudge at the right interval, stopped automatically when the customer replies.</li><li><strong>Job details reach the office the same day</strong> &mdash; photos and notes from site filed against the right job, not lost in a message thread.</li></ul><p>Evidence status: no trade-customer case studies yet either. But the booking automation, SMS scheduling and abandoned-enquiry follow-ups described here are the exact systems we built for ourselves and run in production every day.</p>'}, {'h2': 'Manufacturing, engineering, marine and energy services', 'html': '<p>Workshops, engineering firms, marine services and off-grid energy installers share a shape: physical assets, service history, and equipment that tells you things &mdash; if anything is listening. Job cards get filled in from memory. A machine&rsquo;s history lives in three heads and a filing cabinet. Faults are discovered by the customer rather than by the readings.</p><p>This is the lane where our evidence is strongest, and we&rsquo;ll say so plainly: 365 Techies is a <strong>Victron Recommended Software Integrator</strong>, and we build and run live telemetry dashboards for marine and off-grid energy systems &mdash; real battery, solar and power data from real installations, presented so a person can act on it. The same approach extends to service intelligence for other equipment:</p><ul><li><strong>Job cards become searchable history</strong> &mdash; every visit, part and reading filed against the asset, so the next engineer starts informed.</li><li><strong>Telemetry raises the flag first</strong> &mdash; dashboards and alerts built from live readings, so servicing is triggered by evidence rather than the calendar alone.</li><li><strong>Reports write their own first draft</strong> &mdash; service summaries assembled from the data, checked by an engineer before they go out.</li></ul>'}, {'h2': 'Where our proof comes from', 'html': '<p>We&rsquo;re not going to show you invented case studies, and the honest position is that our AI services are new enough that named customer stories don&rsquo;t exist yet. What does exist: the automations we run in our own business every day &mdash; bookings, reminders, review requests, Slack-connected workflows &mdash; the monitoring dashboards behind our Victron integrator work, and the <a href="/365-ai-os/">365 AI OS demo</a>, a scripted demonstration of a Claude-powered AI desktop we built, labelled honestly as exactly that. Judge us on working systems you can see, not on claims.</p>'}, {'h2': 'The next step is one workflow', 'html': '<p>Pick the single workflow that wastes the most time in your business &mdash; the chasing, the missed calls, the job cards &mdash; and <a href="/contact/">tell us about it</a>. We&rsquo;ll come back with a plain-English plan, a clear line showing what stays human, and a quote. Every AI service runs as a monthly subscription with the design and build quoted separately by complexity, supported by the same local team that looks after your IT.</p>'}], 'faqs': [{'q': 'Can you help if my business is not in one of these industries?', 'a': 'Almost certainly. The lanes above are where the work shows up most often, not a boundary. The underlying workflows &mdash; chasing, scheduling, answering, filing, reporting &mdash; repeat across nearly every small business. Describe yours and we&rsquo;ll tell you honestly whether automation would help.'}, {'q': 'Do you have AI case studies for my industry?', 'a': 'Not yet, and we won&rsquo;t invent any. What we can show you today: the automations we run in our own business every day, genuine telemetry work in the marine and energy lane as a Victron Recommended Software Integrator, and the <a href="/365-ai-os/">365 AI OS</a> demo. As projects complete and clients agree to be named, case studies will appear here.'}, {'q': 'Will we have to replace our existing software?', 'a': 'Usually not. Most of what we build connects to the systems you already run &mdash; Microsoft 365, your accounting package, your job or case management software &mdash; and automates the gaps between them. If a tool genuinely needs replacing, we&rsquo;ll say so and explain why.'}, {'q': 'How is this priced?', 'a': 'Every AI service is a monthly service subscription, with the one-off design and build quoted by complexity &mdash; so you see the full picture before anything starts. <a href="/ai/voice-agents/">AI Voice Agents</a> has published pricing on its own page; for everything else, your quote confirms the monthly figure.'}, {'q': 'Will AI act without a human checking it?', 'a': 'Not where it matters. We design workflows so consequential actions &mdash; sending a quote, replying to a client, committing to a booking &mdash; can wait for human approval, while the routine steps run automatically. You decide where that line sits, and it can move as your confidence grows.'}, {'q': 'Do you cover businesses outside Bournemouth?', 'a': 'Yes. We&rsquo;re based in Bournemouth and work across Poole, Christchurch and the wider Dorset area, with remote delivery UK-wide where it suits the work. Most automation projects are built and supported remotely in any case, with the same family-run team &mdash; trading since 1995 &mdash; accountable for the result.'}], 'final_cta': {'h2': 'Talk to us about your sector&rsquo;s workflow', 'lede': 'Every trade has its own repetitive work. Describe yours and we&rsquo;ll show you what the automation would look like.'}, 'pricing': {'subscription_line': 'Monthly service — confirmed with your quote', 'build_line': 'One-off design & build — quoted by complexity', 'note': 'Your quote confirms both figures before any work begins, and every service is supported by the same local team that looks after your IT.'}},
  {'slug': 'ai/learn', 'name': 'Learn AI', 'title': 'AI Learning Hub: Free Course and Guides | 365 Techies', 'desc': 'Free, plain-English AI learning for business owners: a beginners course, a safe-use guide, and honest answers from a Bournemouth IT team.', 'eyebrow': 'AI &amp; Automation', 'h1': 'Learn AI without the hype', 'hero_lede': 'Free, plain-English learning for business owners &mdash; a beginners course, a practical safety guide, and straight answers from a Bournemouth team that runs these tools in its own business every day.', 'chips': ['Genuinely free course', 'Trading since 1995', '4.9-star Google rating'], 'cta_primary': {'label': 'Talk to us about AI', 'href': '/ai/start/'}, 'cta_secondary': {'label': 'Try the demo', 'href': '/365-ai-os/'}, 'sections': [{'h2': 'Start here: four questions we hear most', 'html': '<p>If you&rsquo;re a business owner wondering whether any of this applies to you, these four answers are the honest place to begin.</p><p><strong>What can AI actually do for a small business?</strong> Today, reliably: draft and tidy written work, summarise long documents, answer repeat questions from your own information, and pull details out of emails and forms so nobody retypes them. Think of it as a capable assistant for well-defined steps &mdash; not a replacement for your judgement, and not something you switch on across the whole business at once.</p><p><strong>What should never go into a chatbot?</strong> Passwords, card details and customers&rsquo; personal information have no place in a public AI tool &mdash; nor does anything commercially sensitive you wouldn&rsquo;t email to an outside contractor. Free tools may use what you type to improve their models. Our <a href="/using-ai-safely/">Using AI Safely guide</a> gives your team simple ground rules.</p><p><strong>When is plain automation better than AI?</strong> When a task follows fixed rules &mdash; send the reminder, copy the record, raise the invoice &mdash; ordinary automation is faster, cheaper and never has an off day. AI earns its place only where a step needs reading, judgement or writing. Most systems we build are mostly straightforward automation, with AI on the one or two steps that genuinely need it &mdash; <a href="/ai/automations/">our automation service</a> shows how that looks in practice.</p><p><strong>How do I know if a process is worth automating?</strong> Three signs: it happens often, it follows a pattern you could explain to a new starter, and it eats time or causes mistakes when it&rsquo;s rushed. If a job ticks all three, it&rsquo;s a strong candidate; if it ticks none, leave it with a human. <a href="/ai/consultancy/">Our AI consultancy</a> exists to work through exactly this question with you.</p>'}, {'h2': 'Using AI safely', 'html': '<p>The AI risks that actually bite small firms are ordinary ones: customer details pasted into a free tool, a confident answer that turned out to be wrong, an email sent without a person reading it first. None of them needs a security department to prevent &mdash; they need a few plain rules everyone in the business understands.</p><p>The <a href="/using-ai-safely/">Using AI Safely guide</a> covers what to keep out of AI tools, how to check answers before you act on them, and where to keep a person in charge of the outcome. It&rsquo;s the same approach we take in the systems we build: on anything consequential, a human approves before anything happens. Plain English throughout, no scare stories, and we keep it current as the tools change.</p>'}, {'h2': 'AI for Beginners: a genuinely free course', 'html': '<p>If you&rsquo;d rather learn properly than piece things together from headlines, the <a href="/ai-for-beginners-course/">AI for Beginners course</a> starts from zero: what these tools actually are, what they&rsquo;re genuinely good at, where they fail, and how to try them without putting anything important at risk. It&rsquo;s written in plain English for people who run a business, not for developers.</p><p>It&rsquo;s genuinely free. We built it because most owners we speak to want to understand AI before they spend a penny on it, and we&rsquo;d rather you arrived at any conversation knowing exactly what you&rsquo;re asking for. Work through it at your own pace &mdash; there&rsquo;s no time limit and nothing to buy at the end.</p>'}, {'h2': 'Learn from systems that actually run', 'html': '<p>Reading only takes you so far, and we&rsquo;d rather show than tell. The <a href="/365-ai-os/">365 AI OS demo</a> is a working, Claude-powered AI desktop we built ourselves &mdash; a scripted demonstration, honestly labelled as one, so you can watch an AI-assisted workflow run rather than imagine it.</p><p>Behind it sit the systems we run on our own business every day: booking automation, review requests, SMS scheduling and Slack-connected workflows, all built in-house. That matters for learning too. These guides are written by the family-run Bournemouth team that has looked after local IT since 1995 and lives with these tools daily &mdash; not by a content agency.</p><p>And when the reading turns into a specific idea &mdash; a task you&rsquo;d like off your team&rsquo;s plate &mdash; <a href="/contact/">tell us what&rsquo;s wasting your team&rsquo;s time</a>. We&rsquo;ll say honestly whether it&rsquo;s an AI job, a plain automation job, or not worth doing at all.</p>'}], 'faqs': [{'q': 'Is the AI for Beginners course really free?', 'a': 'Yes &mdash; free means free. We built it because most owners want to understand AI before they spend anything on it, and an informed customer is easier to help well. If you take the course and never contact us, that&rsquo;s a perfectly good outcome.'}, {'q': 'Do I need a technical background for any of this?', 'a': 'No. Everything here is written in plain English for people who run a business, and jargon is explained the first time it appears. If something still isn&rsquo;t clear, that&rsquo;s our fault rather than yours &mdash; tell us and we&rsquo;ll rewrite it.'}, {'q': 'Is it safe for my staff to use tools like ChatGPT at work?', 'a': 'It can be, with ground rules: keep customer personal data, passwords and payment details out of public tools, and check important answers before acting on them. The <a href="/using-ai-safely/">Using AI Safely guide</a> puts this in a form you can share with your team, and it&rsquo;s a sensible first step before any wider AI plans.'}, {'q': 'Can you help us apply what we learn?', 'a': 'Yes. When reading turns into a specific idea, our <a href="/ai/consultancy/">AI consultancy</a> works out whether a process is worth automating and what it would take, and our automation service builds and runs the result. Both start from the problem, not from a technology choice &mdash; you never need to pick a tool before talking to us.'}, {'q': 'Why is a local IT support company teaching AI?', 'a': 'Because customers kept asking, and because we already build and run AI-assisted systems in our own business every day. We&rsquo;re a family-run Bournemouth firm, trading since 1995 with a 4.9-star Google rating, and explaining technology honestly has always been most of the job.'}], 'final_cta': {'h2': 'Keep learning, then ask us the hard questions', 'lede': 'Start with the free guides and course &mdash; and when a process starts looking automatable, we&rsquo;re here.'}},
]
